---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { EXAMPLES } from '../../lib/cortex/examples.js';
import exampleReport from '../../../docs/cortex/benchmarks/examples-latest.json';
import { evidenceRegistry, parameterRegistry } from '../../lib/cortex/evidence.js';
---
<Layout pageTitle="Cortex Laboratory — methods" subtitle="What is calculated, what is assumed, and what remains unvalidated.">
  <article class="cortex-methods">
    <a href="/cortex/">← Open the laboratory</a>
    <h2>A verified mathematical foundation</h2>
    <p>The laboratory uses the published human temporal active-neuron model for every experiment. The cell shares the implant world and calculates extracellular potentials from total transmembrane currents. Numerical checks establish agreement for stated mathematical cases. This implementation has no independent experimental validation.</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Subsystem</th><th>Current status</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Implant placement</td><td>Implemented; numerically verified</td><td>Shared catalog dimensions and rigid transforms. Tip exposure remains a reconstruction.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Volume conductor</td><td>Numerically verified for stated cases</td><td>Homogeneous unbounded conductor with ideal contacts; near-source exclusions apply.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Active single neuron</td><td>Published reference implemented</td><td>Human temporal reconstructed dendrites, fitted densities and a synthetic axon stub; mixed-species channel kinetics.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cortical anatomy</td><td>Illustrative slab and layers</td><td>One selected neuron; no population density or scan reconstruction.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Populations, acquisition, mechanics</td><td>Outside this milestone</td><td>No population recruitment, measured electronics or deformation model.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Experimental validation</td><td>Not yet performed</td><td>No independent experimental comparison or calibration has been completed.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <h2>One coordinate system</h2>
    <p>Positions use micrometers in a right-handed frame: pia is Z = 0; positive Z points into cortex. Rendering converts every structure uniformly to millimeters. Implant rotations apply X, then Y, then Z about the center of the tissue-facing substrate surface, followed by translation. The scale bar measures distance in the camera-target plane; the coordinate inspector gives exact model coordinates and soma distances.</p>
    <p>The Utah 1.5 mm reference comes directly from the existing device catalog. Its 100 physical shanks and 96 nominal channels are separate fields. The chosen sensing sites are hypothetical ideal probes, not an invented wiring map. Unknown exposed areas and channel assignments stay null. Neither substrate displacement nor electrical contrast is modeled.</p>
    <h2>Membrane and extracellular currents</h2>
    <p>For compartment i, C is total capacitance, Iᵢₒₙ includes leak and active ionic currents, Gᵢⱼ is axial conductance and Vₘ = Vᵢ − φₑ. Positive membrane current points outward. The solver uses mV, ms, nA, nF and µS, with explicit conversions from membrane area and resistivity.</p>
    <pre>Cᵢ dVₘ,ᵢ/dt + Iᵢₒₙ,ᵢ + gₛ,ᵢ(Vₘ,ᵢ − Eₛ)
  = Σⱼ Gᵢⱼ [(Vₘ,ⱼ + φₑ,ⱼ) − (Vₘ,ᵢ + φₑ,ᵢ)] + Iᵢₙⱼ,ᵢ

Iₘ,ᵢ = Cᵢ dVₘ,ᵢ/dt + Iᵢₒₙ,ᵢ + Iₛᵧₙ,ᵢ
Σᵢ Iₘ,ᵢ = Σᵢ Iᵢₙⱼ,ᵢ</pre>
    <p>The human cell has sealed terminals and a prescribed synaptic conductance step on a fixed physical apical region. Synaptic kinetics and placement are assumed inputs.</p>
    <h2 id="active-model">Human temporal active reference</h2>
    <p>The laboratory uses specimen 571654895, a human middle temporal layer-2 spiny cell from Mosher, Wei et al. (2020). The reconstructed dendrites retain native SWC taper, section area and axial resistance. The published directed processor replaces the reconstructed axon with two 30 µm × 1 µm active sections. The source SWC contains a longer reconstructed axon, which is not the simulated axon. Arbitrary placement in this slab does not make the cell a motor-cortex specimen.</p>
    <p>Nine mechanisms—NaV, Ih, Im, K_Pst, K_Tst, Kv3_1, Ca_HVA, Ca_LVA and SK—plus calcium dynamics and leak use the published fitted distributions. Human morphology and fitted densities do not imply measured human channel kinetics: sodium, potassium and M-current kinetics include mouse, rat and bullfrog sources. The temperature is fixed at the source model's 34 °C.</p>
    <p>The membrane equation above includes all active ionic currents alongside capacitive and synaptic terms. Sodium uses the original twelve-state Markov model; other gates use their specified exponential state updates. Voltage uses channel states from the solve, followed by state updates. Exported currents and displayed end-state probabilities therefore have an explicit sampling-phase distinction. Shared zero-area branch junctions carry axial current but have no membrane, channel or recording source.</p>
    <p>Initialization follows the source parameter JSON at −80 mV, then the declared no-drive conditioning period. The example simulation instead starts at −70 mV; that discrepancy is recorded. The port also preserves an upstream NaV initialization-equation discrepancy. Frame zero is the conditioned state, with residual drift reported by no-drive reference cases; exact equilibrium is not assumed.</p>
    <p>Upward crossings of −25 mV are detected at internal solver steps and interpolated in time. Soma events and earliest local compartment crossings are distinct. Extracellular pulses can produce local dendritic crossings without a soma event; crossing location alone does not establish selective stimulation or a biological recruitment threshold.</p>
    <p>The original model was fitted to surgical-slice whole-cell observations; paper Figure 3 reports training error. No held-out biological validation has been performed here. <a href="/models/cortex/allen571654895/571654895.swc">Source morphology</a>, <a href="/models/cortex/allen571654895/optim_param_571654895.json">fitted parameters</a> and <a href="/models/cortex/allen571654895/LICENSE.txt">reuse license</a> are retained. The Allen Institute license permits noncommercial redistribution and includes a commercial redistribution restriction.</p>
    <p>The extracellular point-source solution in an infinite isotropic conductor is φ = Σ Iₘ/(4πσr). The line-source option integrates 1/r uniformly along each cylindrical compartment and uses a point source for the soma. Tissue potentials are referred to infinity or differenced as A − B. The code rejects selected sources or sensing points within 25 µm of the modeled membrane instead of hiding the singularity with a distance clamp. That rule does not prove accuracy outside the excluded region.</p>
    <h2>Three distinct interventions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Synaptic recording:</strong> a conductance event produces membrane currents and an extracellular signal. Total transmembrane current sums to zero.</li>
      <li><strong>Intracellular current clamp:</strong> current enters the soma; an explicit bath return contributes both an applied field and a recording artifact. Net membrane current equals the injected current.</li>
      <li><strong>Extracellular polarization:</strong> two ideal point contacts deliver equal and opposite biphasic currents. The resulting extracellular gradient drives the same cable. A uniform potential offset cannot alter its membrane response.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The neural contribution, applied-source artifact and their sum remain separate. Stimulation and recording cannot use the same ideal source point because its potential is undefined there. Charge per phase is descriptive. Exposed contact area is unknown, so geometric charge density is not supplied.</p>
    <h2 id="electrical-display">Reading the electrical view</h2>
    <p>Four explicit legends distinguish membrane voltage (mV), stimulating-contact outward current (µA), applied extracellular tissue potential (mV), and the selected ideal recording-contact component (µV). Neural potential comes from the forward sum of total membrane currents; applied potential comes from stimulating contacts or the bath return; total is their sum. No neural or total tissue volume is inferred from the two recording traces.</p>
    <p>Stimulating spheres and recording diamonds are enlarged visual guides at the solver's exact contact coordinates. Their labels identify the ordered source/return or recording pair, physical contact ID, current or voltage, and polarity. Positive source current is outward into tissue (anodic); negative is a sink (cathodic). Source A leads cathodic, B supplies the equal/opposite local return, and the signs reverse in the second phase. Each recording marker remains an individual infinity-referenced sample when the plot is switched to A−B.</p>
    <p>Applied slices use the same transformed point sources, conductivity and exported interval-average drive as the experiment. Choose XY, XZ, YZ or three planes, offset their center from the soma, adjust extent/opacity, and select fixed or automatic symmetric scales. Automatic tissue scale uses the largest visible sampled transfer times full pulse amplitude; recording auto-scale uses both contacts over the complete run. Thus neither scale changes during rest. Saturated vertex/contact counts are explicit. Neutral color means zero relative to infinity; blue and red indicate sign, not electric-field magnitude or direction.</p>
    <p>Each plane has 25 × 25 calculated vertices with linear display interpolation. Both triangles of any cell intersecting a source-exclusion sphere are removed, even if no vertex lies inside it. Coarse holes can be larger than the stated sphere. No singularity is hidden by regularizing the distance. Tissue layers, pia and implant bodies do not alter this unbounded homogeneous solution. No electrode surface voltage or impedance is modeled. A new volumetric or isopotential view is deferred until it adds useful, independently checked information beyond these inspectable slices.</p>
    <p>Membrane voltage is at the retained sample time; drive and artifact average only the preceding internal timestep. The replay cursor, readouts, traces and slice values all use that same sample. Phase buttons jump to actual retained negative/positive samples; previous/next stepping can inspect individual intervals. Missing pulse phases at coarse output sampling are reported. Slow replay cannot recover samples that were never retained.</p>
    <h2 id="examples">Reproducible examples</h2>
    <p>Select a named example, read the reset disclosure, load it, inspect placement, then run. Each is a complete immutable configuration; loading clears current results and retains one pinned baseline. Pin the shifted array result and load its unshifted or polarity comparison to inspect the changed configuration fields, soma event times and recording metrics. Exact configuration and result exports are available in the laboratory.</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Example</th><th>Calculated soma events (ms)</th><th>Purpose / scope</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      {EXAMPLES.map(e => { const measured = exampleReport.cases.find(c => c.id === e.id); return <tr><td><a href={`/cortex/?example=${e.id}`}>{e.title}</a></td><td>{measured.somaSpikes.length ? measured.somaSpikes.map(s => s.timeMs.toFixed(6)).join(', ') : 'None'}</td><td>{e.purpose}</td></tr>; })}
    </tbody></table>
    <p>The shifted-array candidate uses the published human cell, 40 µA, 1000 µs phases, 100 µs gap, translation [100, −50, 0] µm, stimulating r4-c4/r4-c5 and recording r3-c3/r3-c6. Its 0.005 ms / 40 µm calculation produces one soma event at approximately 5.381823 ms; the same pulse at [0, 0, 0] produces none. Reversing the ordered source pair produces a later soma event, approximately 6.657215 ms. These strong idealized inputs also produce very large hyperpolarization; none are safe-device recommendations.</p>
    <p>All nine human-cell examples were rerun from exact configurations; measured reports and SHA-256 source/configuration fingerprints are retained in <a href="https://github.com/corbins-cryo-pod/buildthesimulation/blob/main/docs/cortex/EXAMPLES.md">the example verification record</a>. Original native NEURON/LFPykit fixtures cover the short intracellular and 200 µs local-crossing examples. Separate new native fixtures verify both sustained current-step examples, including all saved physical-membrane voltages/currents and ideal recordings; they pass the same numerical agreement tolerances. The new 1000 µs shifted/reversed examples are laboratory-kernel reproductions without new independent native dynamics fixtures. Original model fitting, numerical verification and independent biological validation are distinct.</p>
    <h2 id="current-steps">Sustained current-clamp steps</h2>
    <p>The 200 ms and 500 ms patch-clamp examples inject +0.3 nA (300 pA) at the soma and allow repeated action potentials to emerge from the original channel mechanisms. They produce nine and eighteen soma crossings, respectively; the latter includes seventeen during the step and one immediately after offset. Longer interspike intervals show model-specific adaptation. Count divided by pulse duration is an average count rate, not a constant instantaneous rate.</p>
    <p>Active inputs support up to 1000 ms steps within recordings up to 1200 ms. Saved output may be explicitly spaced up to 0.5 ms to stay under the unchanged 64 MiB budget; integration and spike detection still use the specified internal timestep. Coarser output can miss waveform peaks. Use a shorter recording and finer output to inspect those. These are ideal current-clamp calculations without pipette/access resistance or acquisition electronics, not voltage-clamp experiments.</p>
    <p>Previous/next spike and step-onset/offset buttons navigate retained frames. Exact interpolated event times remain in the spike table and results export. Replay supports up to 100 biological ms/s. A longer replay cannot restore unsaved waveform detail.</p>
    <h2 id="refinement">Refinement and remaining numerical uncertainty</h2>
    <p>A bounded study compares 0.005, 0.0025 and 0.00125 ms timesteps at 40, 20 and 10 µm maximum compartment lengths. Comparisons hold the physical experiment and probe locations fixed, changing timestep or mesh separately. The study checks section topology, membrane area, arc length, capacitance and integrated channel conductance before interpreting numerical differences. It reports spike timing, local crossings, complete soma/selected-probe waveform errors, neural versus artifact errors, conservation, threshold brackets, runtime and numeric output memory.</p>
    <p>Local-crossing thresholds remain spatially sensitive and nonmonotonic across these meshes; passing an independent-solver comparison at one discretization does not establish convergence. Recording-source chords change with refinement, but recording is one-way: chord errors affect recorded potentials and cannot cause a stimulation-threshold shift. The discretized cable, channels and center-sampled applied field jointly determine threshold sensitivity. All tested event counts/timing and applied-artifact comparisons passed, while sharp-spike waveforms failed predeclared voltage-error gates; the shifted candidate also failed one neural-recording error gate. All six extracellular spatial-threshold comparisons failed the 2% envelope criterion. Failed predeclared gates remain visible in <a href="https://github.com/corbins-cryo-pod/buildthesimulation/blob/main/docs/cortex/REFINEMENT.md">the refinement record</a>; no solver or physical model was changed to force a pass.</p>
    <h2>Parameters and evidence</h2>
    <p>The following table describes tissue/display assumptions. Section capacitances, resistivity and channel densities come from the pinned human model parameter file and are retained in result bundles alongside configuration and units. Default integration is 0.005 ms, output sampling is 0.05 ms, maximum segment length is 40 µm and initialization conditioning is 100 ms. These are numerical choices, not empirical confidence intervals.</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Default</th><th>Evidence category / limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>{parameterRegistry.filter(p => ['conductivity', 'thickness', 'exclusion'].includes(p.id)).map(p => <tr><td>{p.id}</td><td>{p.value} {p.unit}</td><td>{p.category}. {p.limitation}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
    <p>The cortical slab is 6 × 6 × 4 mm by default. Layer fractions are 0, 0.06, 0.16, 0.46, 0.50, 0.75 and 1 times thickness. These boundaries, including layer IV, are illustrative. The displayed CSF and white matter do not create different conductivities. Curvature, vessels, meningeal mechanics, neuronal density and excitatory/inhibitory populations are absent.</p>
    <h2>Verification, exports and performance</h2>
    <p>Reproducible tests check SI unit conversions, line-source quadrature, dipole decay, analytic RC and two-compartment responses, sealed-cable polarization, current conservation, gauge invariance, rigid transforms, charge balance, singularity rejection, deterministic runs and exact CSV/JSON agreement.</p>
    <p>Historical kernel checks, retained offline rather than selectable in the laboratory, include independent NEURON 9.0.1 references for a uniform passive cable and the complete declared 48-compartment arbor. The arbor comparison includes all three protocols and a rotated implant with a pulse onset between timesteps. Across fourteen sampled frames per case, voltage agrees within 9 × 10⁻¹² mV and total membrane current within 4 × 10⁻¹³ nA. NEURON builds its own sections and solves their equations; shared inputs contain geometry and parameters only. Its five membrane-free soma connectors reproduce the declared radial-resistance approximation, not a resolved spherical soma.</p>
    <p>LFPykit 0.6.2 independently computes point/line-source coefficients and recordings from those NEURON currents. Eight cases compare neural, applied, total and bipolar signals, agreeing within 3 × 10⁻¹³ µV at sampled times. The tested geometries do not activate LFPykit's near-source radius regularization. These are fixed numerical comparisons, not biological error bars or guarantees for arbitrary configurations. Generators, package versions, exact inputs and hashed fixtures are retained in the repository.</p>
    <p>Active references independently import the source SWC into NEURON 9.0.1 and compile the original NMODL mechanisms. Checks cover held-voltage channel trajectories, no-drive and intracellular responses, extracellular pulses, changed placement, membrane currents and LFPykit recordings. All physical compartments are compared over the complete exported sampling grid, with internal-step crossing times retained separately. Predeclared gates, exact scope, fixture quantization, refinement results and achieved errors are documented in the repository's ACTIVE_MODEL.md and validation report.</p>
    <p>Run <code>node --test tests/cortex-active-reference.mjs tests/cortex-current-step-reference.mjs</code> and <code>node scripts/benchmark-cortex-active.mjs</code> in the repository. The benchmark report records hardware, timings, resolution and convergence. The on-page timer measures the local numerical run, not rendering FPS. Cross-browser speed, full peak memory and GPU performance have not been measured.</p>
    <p>JSON bundles include configuration, engine and catalog revisions, geometry, source registry, frame-major voltages/currents, separated potentials and diagnostics. Active bundles also retain fitted parameters, channel outputs and spike detection conventions. CSV exports the chosen signal component and every compartment current. Frame zero follows declared no-drive conditioning. Active output sampling may be coarser than integration: each exported drive/artifact value averages only the preceding internal timestep. No analog filtering, anti-aliasing or ADC is implied. The laboratory accepts human active-cell configurations using version 2. Historical passive version-1 files are rejected with instructions to load a human-cell example; they are not silently converted. These are experiment schemas, not general SWC, SONATA, NeuroML or NWB importers.</p>
    <h2>Primary sources and scope</h2>
    <ol class="sources">{evidenceRegistry.sources.map(s => <li id={s.id}><a href={s.url}>{s.authors}{s.year ? ` (${s.year})` : ''}: {s.title}</a><p>{s.locator}. {s.doi ? `DOI: ${s.doi}.` : ''} Evidence: {s.species}. {s.limitation}</p></li>)}</ol>
    <h2>Next scientific gate</h2>
    <p>Complete browser review and broaden active parameter/resolution coverage. Compare against independently selected electrophysiology with explicit liquid-junction correction and held-out observations. Realistic axonal extent, population recruitment, multilayer finite-element fields and mechanics require separate modeling and validation. The existing legacy tools remain separate until their models meet the shared geometry and current contracts.</p>
    <p><a href="/cortex/">Return to the laboratory →</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>
